"""Pattern explanations with original examples and language-neutral sketches."""
PATTERN_LESSONS={
4:[
('Factory Method', 'A report exporter needs a writer, but different exporters may choose different formats. Its processing code can call make_writer(), then use the returned writer without choosing the concrete type itself. Factory Method makes that creation step a variation point.\n\nUse it when the creator has a meaningful construction choice to customise. A supplied function may be sufficient; inheritance is not always needed. Abstract Factory deals with a different scale of choice: a compatible family of products rather than one creation operation.', ['Exporter calls factory method', 'Concrete creator selects CSV writer', 'Writer satisfies output contract'], 'export(rows):\n    writer = make_writer()\n    writer.write(rows)'),
('Abstract Factory', 'An HTML notification needs a renderer and a compatible template. Selecting them independently could mix components that expect different content models. Abstract Factory creates the related objects as one family.\n\nThe client asks the selected factory for each collaborator, keeping the family choice together. This helps when the application switches complete families. Adding a new kind of product can require changes to every factory, so there is a maintenance cost. If one stable set of objects is all that is needed, direct construction may be clearer.', ['Select HTML family', 'Create HTML renderer and template', 'Both share compatible content model'], 'family = HtmlNotificationFactory()\nrenderer = family.renderer()\ntemplate = family.template()\nrenderer.render(template, data)'),
('Builder', 'A notification requires a recipient and template, with optional locale and expiry. A builder collects these choices and checks them before creating the usable notification.\n\nThe final build step should reject missing or incompatible fields. Until then, the partially assembled value should not be treated as ready to send. Builder is useful when staged construction improves clarity. For a small value, a constructor with named parameters may already express everything safely. Its purpose is controlled assembly, rather than creating a family of different products.', ['Set recipient and template', 'Add optional expiry', 'Build validates required fields'], 'builder.to(recipient).template(template_id)\nnotification = builder.expires_at(deadline).build()\n# build rejects missing recipient or incompatible fields'),
('Prototype', 'A report layout contains a carefully configured set of values. Prototype creates another layout by copying that configured instance rather than rebuilding every setting.\n\nSpecify what the copy owns. A shallow copy can leave both layouts referring to the same mutable object; a deeper copy may duplicate more state. Copying a connection handle does not create an independently owned connection. Shared immutable font data can remain shared, while editable layout values need suitable ownership. Use copying when those rules are clearer than constructing fresh request state from immutable configuration.', ['Configured layout prototype', 'Copy layout-owned values', 'Do not duplicate resource ownership'], 'layout = prototype.clone_owned_values()\nlayout.title = "Monthly report"\n# Shared immutable font data remains shared'),
('Singleton', 'An application may load one immutable configuration object and share it with its services. A Singleton arrangement restricts access to an instance within a stated program or container boundary. Another process may still have its own instance.\n\nKeep that boundary and ownership explicit. Shared mutable request state can cause races and test interference. Container-managed singleton lifetime and a class offering hidden global access are related but different arrangements. Passing an application-owned instance to its users often makes dependencies easier to see and tests easier to isolate.', ['Process A has instance S1', 'Process B has instance S2', 'Global uniqueness is not implied'], 'configuration = load_immutable_configuration()\nservice = Service(configuration)\n# One application-owned instance; no hidden global lookup'),
],
5:[
('Adapter', "An SMS SDK returns provider-specific status codes. The application's Sender contract expects accepted, declined, or uncertain with a reference. An Adapter translates between these interfaces.\n\nThe translation must preserve meaning. A provider timeout should not become a definite decline just because that is easier to encode. Keep SDK-specific types inside the adapter where practical, and test its mapping against the provider contract. Decorator usually adds behaviour around the same interface; Adapter's main job is to reconcile an interface or representation mismatch.", ['Application Sender contract', 'Translate request and outcomes', 'Foreign SMS provider API'], 'send(message):\n    raw = sdk.submit(encode(message))\n    return map_provider_outcome(raw)'),
('Bridge', 'Suppose notifications vary by content—alerts or receipts—and by transport—email or SMS. Subclassing every combination produces many similar classes. A Bridge lets each content object use a transport contract through composition.\n\nThe two dimensions can then vary independently. This needs a well-chosen shared contract and some wiring. Adapter usually connects an existing mismatch; Bridge deliberately separates variations in the design. It is useful when those variations are real, rather than when one fixed sender would serve the application more simply.', ['Alert or receipt content', 'Composes transport interface', 'Email or SMS transport'], 'class Receipt:\n    constructor(transport): self.transport = transport\n    deliver(data): return self.transport.send(format_receipt(data))'),
('Composite', 'A notification audience can be one user or a group containing other audiences. Composite gives leaves and groups a compatible interface so a caller can traverse the audience structure.\n\nDefine duplicates, cycles, depth or size limits, and how failures are reported. A send to a group can partly succeed even if its method looks like a send to one user. The shared interface does not make the whole group atomic. Use a simple collection loop when the domain has no useful tree-shaped structure.', ['Audience group contains audiences', 'Leaf is one authorized recipient', 'Traversal bounds and deduplicates'], 'recipients(node, seen):\n    if node.id in seen: return []\n    seen.add(node.id)\n    return node.users_or_recursive_children(seen)'),
('Decorator', 'A metrics wrapper records how long Sender.send takes, then returns its result. Decorator adds behaviour around a collaborator while retaining its interface.\n\nOrder can change meaning: placing metrics inside a retry wrapper counts attempts, while placing it outside can measure one logical call. Retrying also changes the risk of duplicate effects, so it needs a safe repetition contract. Use decorators where composition makes such behaviour clear. Avoid burying essential business decisions in a long wrapper stack that callers cannot easily inspect.', ['Metrics wraps Sender', 'Sender returns explicit outcome', 'Wrapper records duration and result'], 'send(message):\n    start = clock()\n    try: return inner.send(message)\n    finally: metrics.observe(clock() - start)'),
('Facade', 'Submitting a notification may involve validation, template selection, and saving a delivery plan. A Facade offers one focused operation that coordinates this subsystem for its clients.\n\nThe caller no longer needs to know every internal step. The response must still distinguish accepted work from completed delivery. Keep the facade focused; accumulating every unrelated operation turns it into a large coordinator that is difficult to change. A Proxy commonly stands in for the same target interface, while a Facade primarily offers a simpler entry to several cooperating parts.', ['Client uses one submit operation', 'Facade coordinates subsystem roles', 'Returns accepted intent identity'], 'submit(command):\n    validated = validate(command)\n    return intents.persist(render_plan(validated))'),
('Flyweight', 'Thousands of notifications may use one parsed template. Flyweight shares that reusable, immutable part while each rendering supplies its own recipient and variables.\n\nSeparating shared content from per-use context reduces repeated storage. Mixing recipient state into the shared template could leak data between users. Measure whether memory pressure justifies extra pooling or caching; ordinary immutable reuse may be enough. Sharing also leaves version selection, cache expiry, and resource lifetime to be designed. The pattern identifies what can be shared, rather than solving every cache-management problem.', ['Shared immutable template parse', 'Recipient A context separate', 'Recipient B context separate'], 'template = parsed_templates.get(version)\noutput_a = render(template, context_a)\noutput_b = render(template, context_b)'),
('Proxy', "Before reading a document, a Proxy can check whether the actor currently has access, then forward the call to the target. It presents the target's interface while controlling access or another aspect of its use.\n\nOther proxies defer creation or represent a remote target. A remote call can fail or take substantial time, so its contract must expose the relevant uncertainty. Avoid interfaces that look cheap while hiding unbounded network retries or loads. A Facade simplifies subsystem operations; a Proxy usually represents the same conceptual target with controlled access.", ['Client requests document read', 'Proxy checks current access', 'Authorized target returns data'], 'read(document_id, actor):\n    authorize(actor, document_id)\n    return target.read(document_id)'),
],
6:[
('Strategy', "An order's price can be calculated under different pricing policies. Strategy supplies one calculation behind a contract, letting the caller use the selected policy without embedding every algorithm.\n\nStrategies must agree on amounts, units, errors, and effects. If there is only one stable formula, a function may be sufficient. Strategy varies a selected policy; State varies behaviour according to an entity's lifecycle and history. Choose the former when a policy is meant to be interchangeable, rather than letting a caller arbitrarily replace a payment's valid lifecycle state.", ['Policy selects strategy', 'Caller invokes stable operation', 'Algorithm returns defined result'], 'price = pricing_strategy.calculate(order)\nrequire(price.currency == order.currency)'),
('Observer', 'A subject can notify several subscribed observers when something changes. Observer is useful for one-to-many reactions without making the subject call each concrete implementation directly.\n\nDecide whether callbacks run inline, in what order, and what happens if one throws or runs slowly. Remove subscriptions when their owners end so they do not retain unwanted objects. A local callback list exists only in process memory. When a required reaction must survive process loss, use an appropriate durable publication mechanism rather than treating local observation as a message broker.', ['Subject changes state', 'Subscribed observers notified', 'Failure policy defines isolation'], 'for observer in snapshot(subscribers):\n    notify_under_defined_failure_policy(observer, event)'),
('Command', "SendReceipt(order_id, operation_id) records an intention that a handler can execute later. Command gives a requested action explicit data and identity. An event instead records a fact that has already been accepted.\n\nFor durable work, prefer serialisable domain data over a closure holding a request's live connections. At execution time, the handler must check current validity and the chosen authorisation policy. Retries need duplicate handling. Representing an intention makes scheduling and audit easier, but storing a command alone does not establish safe repeated external execution.", ['Command: send receipt', 'Queue stores versioned intention', 'Handler validates and executes'], 'command = SendReceipt(order_id, operation_id)\nhandler.handle(command)\n# Handler checks current authorization and state'),
('State', "A pending payment and a captured payment accept different actions. State organises behaviour around that lifecycle. A guarded transition changes the state when the required evidence arrives.\n\nA small transition table can be clearer than a class for every state. Whichever representation is used, persist related state changes atomically and keep invalid transitions explicit. A caller cannot simply select “captured” as if choosing an interchangeable algorithm: the payment's history and evidence constrain it. This distinguishes lifecycle-dependent State from a selected Strategy.", ['Pending payment', 'Verified settlement transition', 'Captured state permits refund request'], 'require((payment.state, event.kind) in allowed_transitions)\npayment.state = next_state(payment.state, event)'),
('Chain of Responsibility', 'A request passes through authentication, rate limiting, and parsing before reaching the application. A Chain of Responsibility gives each handler defined choices: reject, handle, transform, or continue.\n\nSpecify the order and continuation rules. Forgetting to invoke the next handler can silently swallow work; placing expensive parsing before admission can change cost and exposure. A chain is a coordination structure, not a durable workflow protocol. If several stages must complete across crashes, they also need persistent progress and recovery rules.', ['Authenticate', 'Apply request budget', 'Parse and invoke next handler'], 'auth(rate_limit(parse(application)))(request)\n# Each handler has explicit rejection and continuation rules'),
('Template Method', 'Every import job must validate input, transform it, and commit the result in that order. Template Method fixes the overall sequence while allowing selected steps, such as format conversion, to vary.\n\nThe base process must preserve its required checks when subclasses customise a step. Inheritance is a common implementation, but independent variations may be easier to express through composed strategies. Use the pattern when a stable process really has controlled extension points. Letting subclasses bypass validation or commit ordering undermines the very sequence the template was meant to protect.', ['Fixed validate step', 'Overridable transform step', 'Fixed commit and cleanup'], 'run(input):\n    validate(input)\n    result = transform(input)\n    return commit(result)'),
('Iterator', 'A caller asks an Iterator for the next item without needing to know whether it comes from an array, tree, or database cursor. The iterator keeps the traversal position.\n\nA lazy database iterator can also hold a connection. A slow reader may keep that resource open, while closing its owner too early can make the next read fail. Define closing, end-of-stream, ordering, and behaviour under mutation. For a small bounded result, materialising a list and releasing the connection before returning may provide a simpler contract.', ['Client requests next item', 'Iterator owns traversal position', 'End or close releases resources'], 'with open_bounded_cursor(query) as rows:\n    for row in rows:\n        consume(row)'),
('Mediator', 'Inventory and payments need coordinated steps during checkout. A Mediator can receive their results and decide the next action, reducing direct knowledge between the collaborators.\n\nGive the coordinator a focused responsibility and explicit failure handling. Otherwise, it can become a large object that knows every detail of the application. In a distributed setting, a durable workflow orchestrator can carry this coordination across restarts, but the object pattern alone supplies no persistence. Separate the coordination idea from the mechanism needed to recover its progress.', ['Inventory reports reservation', 'Mediator decides next action', 'Payment collaborator receives command'], 'reservation = inventory.reserve(command)\noutcome = payments.authorize(command.operation_id)\ncoordinate_outcomes(reservation, outcome)'),
('Memento', 'Before an edit, a draft captures the values needed to restore its earlier local state. Memento provides such a supported snapshot without giving every caller access to arbitrary internals.\n\nDefine which values are copied and what restoration means. References to shared mutable objects can make a supposed snapshot change later. Restoring the draft also cannot retract an email or reverse a payment already sent elsewhere. Use the pattern where local capture and restoration are well defined; external compensation, backups, and event histories address different recovery needs.', ['Capture local draft snapshot', 'Apply editable change', 'Restore supported local state'], 'before = draft.snapshot_owned_values()\ndraft.apply(edit)\ndraft.restore(before)\n# No claim about undoing external effects'),
('Visitor', 'A document tree contains known node types such as text and image. A Visitor supplies an operation, such as export or validation, that handles each supported node type.\n\nThis makes adding operations easier without placing every operation on every node. Adding a new node kind can require updating all visitors, so the trade-off reverses for structural change. Pattern matching may be a simpler expression in some languages. Make unknown-node handling explicit so a new document version does not silently lose content during export.', ['Known document node variants', 'Visitor supplies export operation', 'Each node dispatches appropriate behavior'], 'visit(node):\n    match node.kind:\n        case "text": return export_text(node)\n        case "image": return export_image(node)\n        case _: raise UnsupportedNode'),
],
7:[
('Repository', "A reservation service needs to load and save reservations while preserving their rules. Repository provides application-oriented data operations and keeps SQL or other storage details in an adapter.\n\nState transaction ownership, error outcomes, query bounds, and returned-value lifetimes. A repository per table may not match an aggregate's required atomic boundary. Avoid concealing expensive remote calls or live cursors behind a misleadingly simple name. Use the boundary where it hides useful knowledge, and keep real storage integration checks: a test substitute cannot demonstrate actual commit and recovery behaviour.", ['Domain operation', 'Repository contract', 'Concrete persistence adapter'], 'reservation = repository.get_for_update(reservation_id)\nreservation.confirm(payment_evidence)\nrepository.save(reservation)'),
('Unit of Work', 'Reserving inventory and inserting the local reservation should either both commit or both fail. Unit of Work groups these changes under one explicit commit decision and owns their transaction resources.\n\nAll participating local repositories must respect that owner instead of committing independently. Keep the unit short and arrange rollback and cleanup. Calling a remote payment provider inside the same language scope does not make its charge part of the database transaction. External effects need their own protocol and recovery. The useful boundary is the actual transaction, rather than the indentation of the code.', ['Begin local transaction', 'Apply related local changes', 'Commit both or roll back'], 'with unit_of_work() as uow:\n    uow.inventory.reserve(seat)\n    uow.reservations.add(record)\n    uow.commit()'),
('Specification', 'A refund policy can express “this payment is eligible at this time” as a named predicate. Specification makes such a selection or eligibility rule reusable and, where useful, composable.\n\nThe predicate describes a decision based on particular state. That state can change before a refund is applied, so the atomic transition must recheck the relevant version or conditions. When translating a specification into SQL, preserve time, null, and access semantics. A plain function is often enough; add an expression framework only when genuine composition needs justify it.', ['Evaluate refund predicate', 'Concurrent state may change', 'Atomic transition rechecks invariant'], 'eligible = RefundPolicy.satisfied_by(payment, now)\nif eligible:\n    repository.refund_if_still_eligible(payment.version)'),
('CQRS', 'Capturing a payment needs a model that protects allowed transitions. Displaying an account statement may need a different, read-friendly shape. CQRS separates these command and query models when their needs justify it.\n\nA projection can build the read model from accepted changes, but then it may lag and must be rebuildable. State that freshness contract. Both models can exist in one process and one database; event sourcing and separate services are optional choices. When one simple model already serves both uses, the additional synchronisation and maintenance may not be worthwhile.', ['Command enforces invariant', 'Projection updates read model', 'Query serves explicit freshness'], 'commands.capture(payment_id, operation_id)\nstatement = queries.account_statement(account_id)\n# Projection lag is part of the query contract'),
],
}
